## Local Ollama Item analysis

1. Start the Ollama desktop service, or run `ollama serve` if it is not running.
2. Run `ollama list` to find the exact installed DeepSeek 8B tag. Verify service
   availability with `Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:11434/api/tags`.
3. In backend/.env set OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434 and OLLAMA_MODEL to
   that exact tag. Set USE_MOCK_SOURCE=true to avoid real source calls. No model
   is downloaded automatically. Restart backend after configuration changes.
4. From backend with its virtual environment activated, run:
   `python -m alembic upgrade head`, then `python -m alembic current` (0003 head),
   then `uvicorn app.main:app --reload`.
5. In /docs create/scan a Mock Topic and GET /api/topics/{id}/items to obtain an ID.
6. POST /api/items/{item_id}/analyze. Inspect ai_relevant, ai_relevance_score,
   ai_category, ai_summary and ai_analyzed_at in its response and the Items GET.
7. Optionally POST /api/topics/{id}/analyze?limit=1 to analyze another unprocessed
   Item. Start small: local models may be slow. Single calls time out at 120 seconds.
8. Inspect stored results in pgAdmin with:
   `SELECT id, ai_relevant, ai_relevance_score, ai_category, ai_summary, ai_analyzed_at FROM items;`

Automated AI tests use FakeAI and httpx.MockTransport; they never invoke real
Ollama. Existing test commands also cover schema validation, migration and failure
paths. Optional PostgreSQL checks validate AI field persistence and score constraints.
No AI frontend controls have been added; use Swagger for this stage.
Structured output reference: https://docs.ollama.com/capabilities/structured-outputs

## Digest and automatic workflow

1. Configure USE_MOCK_SOURCE=true and SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false in backend/.env.
2. Start Ollama, check `ollama list`, and set OLLAMA_MODEL to an installed local
   model's exact name. OLLAMA_BASE_URL defaults to http://localhost:11434.
3. Activate backend/.venv; run `python -m alembic upgrade head`,
   `python -m alembic current` (0004 head), and `python -m alembic check`.
4. Start backend with `uvicorn app.main:app` and frontend with `npm run dev`.
5. Create a Topic, Scan, and Analyze. At least one Item must be marked relevant;
   model judgments can legitimately leave none. Generate Digest then shows 409.
6. Click Generate Digest. Read the AI-generated label, title, count, time and summary.
   View Digest History loads saved snapshots. API equivalents are POST
   /api/topics/{id}/digest and GET /api/topics/{id}/digests.
7. Restart the backend and reload history to verify persistence.
8. For automation, create a fresh enabled Topic with a unique query, then stop the
   backend. Set SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true and SCAN_INTERVAL_MINUTES=1 for a local test.
   Restart with `uvicorn app.main:app` (one process, no --reload/--workers).
9. Wait one minute plus inference time. Logs show Topic ID, scan counts, analysis
   counts and Digest created/skipped. Refresh Items and View Digest History.
10. A second unchanged Mock scan creates no new Items, reanalyzes none and skips
    Digest generation. Only enabled Topics are processed. Previous pending Items
    can be analyzed in later runs, up to 20 per Topic per cycle.

Safe shutdown: press Ctrl+C and wait for the current external request to finish
or time out (Ollama timeout 120 seconds). No next Item/Topic is started after the
stop signal. Set SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false and restart to keep manual APIs available.
Environment variables override .env; clear or change an existing process-level
SCHEDULER_ENABLED setting too. Interval accepts 0.1-10080 minutes, default 60.
The first run waits the full interval and each next run waits after completion.
Do not run multiple backend processes or manual workflows concurrently with the
scheduler. It is a single-process local feature, with no distributed locking.

Offline tests need neither Ollama nor YouTube. From backend:
```powershell
python -m pytest tests/test_digests.py tests/test_scheduler.py -q -p no:cacheprovider --basetemp "$env:TEMP\sr-digest-$([guid]::NewGuid().ToString('N'))"
$env:RUN_POSTGRES_TESTS='1'
python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider --basetemp "$env:TEMP\sr-all-$([guid]::NewGuid().ToString('N'))"
Remove-Item Env:\RUN_POSTGRES_TESTS
```
These use FakeAI/MockTransport and direct run_cycle calls, never permanent scheduler
threads. Tests forcibly suppress scheduler startup even if local .env enables it.
MockSource supplies fake content, not fake AI: the normal browser workflow still
requires Ollama. Scheduler Digest failure after successful analysis can be recovered
with manual Generate Digest; no persistent retry queue is implemented.

## Frontend startup

In another terminal, run npm run dev from frontend/. Topic management remains at
http://localhost:3000/topics. Use Scan to collect and View items / Refresh items
to read saved results.

## Offline MockSource (development and tests only)

Set `USE_MOCK_SOURCE=true` in backend/.env and restart the backend, or set
`$env:USE_MOCK_SOURCE = 'true'` in its PowerShell terminal before starting uvicorn.
Process environment variables take precedence over .env. No YouTube key is needed.
Unset/default or false uses the existing YouTube source. To switch back, set false
in the active configuration and restart. MockSource is not a real information source.

1. Start local PostgreSQL and the backend, then run npm run dev in frontend/.
2. Create a Topic named Christopher Nolan (use a new name for a clean test).
3. Click Scan: expect source=mock, fetched=3, created=3, duplicates=0 in the response.
4. The Topic automatically displays three simulated Items with mock source labels.
5. Scan again: expect fetched=3, created=0, duplicates=3.
6. Refresh or restart the backend: the saved Items remain available.

IDs derive from a normalized query and fixture index, so repeated scans are stable.
The existing global (source, external_id) constraint also deduplicates across Topics
with the same query; an already scanned query may therefore return duplicates on
the first scan of a new Topic. No schema change is needed. Published dates are fixed
fixtures; links point to localhost placeholders, not genuine articles.

MockSource creates data in memory without an HTTP client. The frontend calls only
the local backend; the backend connects to local PostgreSQL. In browser DevTools,
inspect Scan and Items requests: their destination is 127.0.0.1:8000. External links
are not loaded automatically. Mock tests reject non-loopback socket connections and
fail if YouTubeSource.search is invoked. PostgreSQL integration tests roll back rows.

```powershell
$env:RUN_POSTGRES_TESTS = '1'
python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider --basetemp "$env:TEMP\signalradar-mock-tests-$([guid]::NewGuid().ToString('N'))"
Remove-Item Env:\RUN_POSTGRES_TESTS
```
